{"author":{"id":"7c5527b9befade3b1be0c1d677ac996815288d8332f34269730a5b81425ee5be"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791272047,"nonce":"KQ7B0w++hzHxxQKYoEqWnXu4QDU=","metadata":{"github-url":"https://github.com/EPFL-ENAC/vps-mitsi/issues/44","origin":"github","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:id":"I_kwDOULtcMc8AAAABVTiiXw","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:labels":"","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:number":"44","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:status":"open","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:text":"4eb090807bd4a5dd284a82234ebbac27fe0e9ce92f34aca2b5e3fd9e49f33816","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:title":"[UX](Scope) The app says Scope 'unlocks' the other pages, but nothing is locked","sync:github.com/epfl-enac/vps-mitsi:url":"https://github.com/EPFL-ENAC/vps-mitsi/issues/44"},"title":"[UX](Scope) The app says Scope 'unlocks' the other pages, but nothing is locked","message":"## What users experience\n- The Welcome page says the Scope block is \"mandatory in order to unlock the other blocks\".\n- Inventory, Energy and Results each show a yellow banner: \"Complete the Scope of the assessment block first to unlock the fields.\"\n- **But nothing is locked.** Every field stays editable. The only real effect is that the footer totals show \"—\" until Scope is complete.\n\nThe app promises a rule it doesn't apply. Users either learn to ignore a warning that looks wrong, or conclude the app is broken.\n\n## Why \"Scope first\" still matters\nThere is a real dependency:\n- inventory rows must point to a **datacenter**, and energy is entered **per datacenter**, both defined in Scope;\n- results need the **functional unit** and **lifespan** from Scope.\n\nSo the question isn't whether Scope comes first, but how strictly the app enforces it.\n\n## Options (PM decision)\n- **A. Guide, don't lock.** This fits the \"loose wizard\" direction in #21 and #35. One shared message, for example \"Start with Scope: the other pages use its datacenters and functional unit\", with a link to Scope. The word \"unlock\" goes away everywhere.\n- **B. Lock.** Inventory, Energy and Results are disabled in the navigation until Scope is complete, with the reason shown.\n- **C. Lock only what really depends on Scope.** Datacenter fields stay disabled until a datacenter exists, and Results show a \"needs Scope\" placeholder; everything else stays editable.\n\n## Whichever option is chosen\n- The \"Scope complete\" rule and its message are defined **once**, in the navigation or layout, not as a separate banner and text on each page.\n- The wording matches what the app actually does.\n\n## Done when\n- [ ] The chosen behaviour is applied from a single place.\n- [ ] No text promises a lock that doesn't exist.\n- [ ] A test covers the app with Scope incomplete and with Scope complete.\n\n## Technical notes\n- Banners: `HardwareInventoryPage.vue`, `EnergyConsumptionPage.vue`, `ResultsPage.vue` and `ScopePage.vue`, with texts `inventoryNoScopeHint`, `energyNoScopeHint`, `resultsNoScopeHint`, `scopeBlockFirstHint` and `welcomeStepScopeHint`.\n- The rule itself is `isScopeValid` in `frontend/src/stores/mitsi.ts`: Scope fields are valid and at least one datacenter has an abbreviation and a name.\n- The footer dashes come from `embodiedText`, `operationalText` and `totalLifespanText` in `MainLayout.vue`.\n- Related: #21 (tab vs wizard), #35 (navigation bar), #32 (completion status).","files":null}]}